{% assign sc = site.data.policy_feasibility_scenarios %}
{% assign ref = sc.reference %}
{% assign c_t0 = sc.cases | where: 'id', 'T0' | first %}
{% assign c_t1 = sc.cases | where: 'id', 'T1' | first %}
{% assign c_t1b = sc.cases | where: 'id', 'T1b' | first %}
{% assign c_t2 = sc.cases | where: 'id', 'T2' | first %}
<section id="pf-model" class="pf-section pf-reveal">
  <p class="pf-step-tag">Scenario use</p>
  <h2><span class="pf-section-number">05</span> Bring the assessment into the scenario</h2>

  {% for paragraph in pf.model_application.setting %}
    <p class="pf-prose">{{ paragraph }}</p>
  {% endfor %}
  <div class="pf-settings">
    <div class="pf-setting">
      <p class="pf-setting-label">Adopted-policy scenario</p>
      <ul>
        {% for t in pf.trajectories %}
          {% if t.scenario_use == 'Baseline' or t.scenario_use == 'Sensitivity' %}
            <li data-trajectory="{{ t.id }}">
              <span class="pf-swatch" aria-hidden="true"></span><strong>{{ t.scenario_use }}</strong>
              {{ t.id }}
              {{ t.short_title }}
            </li>
          {% endif %}
        {% endfor %}
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="pf-setting">
      <p class="pf-setting-label">Exploratory scenario</p>
      <ul>
        {% for t in pf.trajectories %}
          {% if t.scenario_use == 'Exploratory' %}
            <li data-trajectory="{{ t.id }}">
              <span class="pf-swatch" aria-hidden="true"></span><strong>{{ t.scenario_use }}</strong> {{ t.id }}
              {{ t.short_title }}
            </li>
          {% endif %}
        {% endfor %}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="pf-quantify">
    <h3>Model runs for {{ sc.model.result_year }}</h3>
    <p class="pf-prose">
      To explore what the choice of trajectory can mean in a model, I ran all four trajectories in a power system model of the Nordic countries for
      {{ sc.model.result_year }}, together with a reference run without new datacenters in the Nordics.
    </p>
    {% assign ds = sc.dc_scenarios %}
    {% capture tracker_link %}<a href="{{ '/projects/datacenter-tracker/' | relative_url }}">Nordic datacenter tracker</a>{% endcapture %}
    {% capture epri_link %}<a href="{{ ds.epri.url }}">{{ ds.epri.label }}</a>{% endcapture %}
    <div class="pf-dcscen">
      <p>{{ ds.lead | replace: 'Nordic datacenter tracker', tracker_link }}</p>
      <p class="pf-caption">Added annual electricity use in 2035, relative to 2025, in TWh a year. Grid-side capacity in GW in brackets.</p>
      <div class="pf-table-scroll">
        <table class="pf-dcscen-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Scenario</th>
              {% for col in ds.columns %}
                <th scope="col">{{ col }}</th>
              {% endfor %}
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {% for r in ds.rows %}
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">
                  {{ r.label -}}
                  <small>{{ r.use }}</small>
                </th>
                {% for v in r.values %}
                  <td>
                    {{ v.twh }}
                    <span>({{ v.gw }} GW)</span>
                  </td>
                {% endfor %}
              </tr>
            {% endfor %}
            {% assign g = ds.grid_row %}
            <tr class="pf-dcscen-grid">
              <th scope="row">
                {{ g.label -}}
                <small>{{ g.use }}</small>
              </th>
              {% for v in g.values %}
                <td>{{ v }}</td>
              {% endfor %}
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <details class="pf-dcscen-notes">
        <summary>Notes</summary>
        <p class="pf-caption">
          {{ ds.caption }}
          {{ ds.grid_note }} Sources:
          {% for src in ds.grid_sources -%}
            <a href="{{ src.url }}">{{ src.label }}</a>
            {%- unless forloop.last %}, {% endunless -%}
          {%- endfor -%}
          .
        </p>
      </details>
    </div>

    {% assign smax = 250.0 %}
    <h4 class="pf-results-head">Results</h4>
    <p class="pf-caption">The model is currently in development and results are work in progress.</p>
    <p class="pf-prose">
      In SE3, the average price is {{ ref.values.price_se3 | round }} EUR/MWh in the reference run and {{ c_t0.values.price_se3 | round }} EUR/MWh
      under both T0 and T1. T1 makes almost no difference because the requirement is mostly met with onshore wind and solar that the model builds
      anyway. Under T1b and T2 the average price is {{ c_t1b.values.price_se3 | round }} EUR/MWh, and prices in the most expensive hours are much
      lower.
    </p>
    <figure class="pf-bars pf-price" aria-labelledby="pf-bars-caption">
      <figcaption id="pf-bars-caption" class="pf-sr-only">Electricity price in SE3, {{ sc.model.result_year }}</figcaption>
      <p class="pf-mix-legend">
        <span><span class="pf-key pf-price-avg" aria-hidden="true"></span>Average spot price</span>
        <span><span class="pf-key pf-price-p99" aria-hidden="true"></span>Most expensive 1% of hours</span>
      </p>
      <div class="pf-price-row pf-price-head" aria-hidden="true">
        <span></span><span>Spot price, SE3 · <span class="pf-nowrap">EUR/MWh</span></span
        ><span></span>
      </div>
      {% assign rows = sc.cases | unshift: ref %}
      {% for c in rows %}
        {% assign t = pf.trajectories | where: 'id', c.id | first %}
        <div class="pf-price-row{% if c.id == 'none' %} pf-bar-reference{% endif %}" data-trajectory="{{ c.id }}">
          <span class="pf-bar-label">
            {%- if t -%}
              <span class="pf-swatch" aria-hidden="true"></span><small>{{ c.id }}</small><span class="pf-mix-name">{{ t.short_title }}</span>
            {%- else -%}
              <span class="pf-mix-short">Ref.</span><span class="pf-mix-name">{{ ref.label }}</span>
            {%- endif -%}
          </span>
          {% assign wa = c.values.price_se3 | times: 100.0 | divided_by: smax %}
          {% assign wp = c.values.p99_se3 | times: 100.0 | divided_by: smax %}
          <span class="pf-price-track">
            <span class="pf-price-p99" style="width: {{ wp }}%"></span>
            <span class="pf-price-avg" style="width: {{ wa }}%"></span>
            <span class="pf-price-num pf-price-num-avg" style="left: {{ wa }}%">{{ c.values.price_se3 | round }}</span>
            <span class="pf-price-num pf-price-num-p99" style="left: {{ wp }}%">{{ c.values.p99_se3 }}</span>
          </span>
        </div>
      {% endfor %}
      {% assign st = sc.stress %}
      <div class="pf-price-row pf-price-stress" data-trajectory="{{ st.id }}">
        <span class="pf-bar-label"
          ><span class="pf-swatch" aria-hidden="true"></span><small>{{ st.id }}</small><span class="pf-mix-name">{{ st.label }}</span
          ><span class="pf-mix-short">{{ st.short }}</span></span
        >
        <span class="pf-price-track">
          {%- if st.values.price_se3 -%}
            {% assign wa = st.values.price_se3 | times: 100.0 | divided_by: smax %}
            {% assign wp = st.values.p99_se3 | times: 100.0 | divided_by: smax %}
            <span class="pf-price-p99" style="width: {{ wp }}%"></span>
            <span class="pf-price-avg" style="width: {{ wa }}%"></span>
            <span class="pf-price-num pf-price-num-avg" style="left: {{ wa }}%">{{ st.values.price_se3 | round }}</span>
            <span class="pf-price-num pf-price-num-p99" style="left: {{ wp }}%">{{ st.values.p99_se3 }}</span>
          {%- else -%}
            <span class="pf-price-pending">{{ st.note }}</span>
          {%- endif -%}
        </span>
      </div>
      <div class="pf-price-row pf-price-axis" aria-hidden="true">
        <span></span>
        <span class="pf-price-ticks">
          {%- for v in (0..2) -%}
            {% assign tv = v | times: 100 -%}
            <span style="left: {{ tv | times: 100.0 | divided_by: smax }}%">{{ tv }}</span>
          {%- endfor -%}
        </span>
      </div>
      <p class="pf-caption">
        Modelled spot price in bidding zone SE3, central Sweden including Stockholm: the yearly average and the price exceeded in only 1% of the
        hours. The trajectories are run with the Central datacenter scenario. The last row is the stress test, in which every announced project is
        built and there is no policy response. All runs, including T2, have the Central scenario’s new datacenters in Norway and Finland. The
        reference has none, which is why T2 is above the reference.
      </p>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div id="pf-additionality" class="pf-callout">
    <h3>Is new generation additional?</h3>
    <p>
      In all runs, the model builds onshore wind in Sweden up to, or very close to, its limit, which corresponds to 70 TWh a year in an average year.
      It does so mainly to meet growing electricity demand. In the Swedish Energy Agency’s current-policy scenario, which the model uses for demand,
      Swedish electricity use grows by about 60 TWh between 2025 and 2035, and about 46 TWh of that comes from industry. The new datacenters in the
      Central scenario add about 17 TWh. Under T1, the requirement is therefore met mostly with onshore wind and solar that the model builds anyway.
      In T0 and T1, most of the added electricity use is covered by imports. T1b counts only generation that the model would not otherwise build. In
      that run the model adds about 15 TWh of offshore wind, and Sweden goes from importing to exporting a little. The results thus depend on how a
      requirement is specified, not only on whether there is one.
    </p>

    {% assign dm = sc.mix.delta %}
    <figure class="pf-mix" aria-labelledby="pf-mix-caption">
      <figcaption id="pf-mix-caption">{{ dm.title }}</figcaption>
      <p class="pf-mix-legend">
        {% for k in dm.carriers %}
          <span><span class="pf-key pf-mix-{{ k.id }}" aria-hidden="true"></span>{{ k.label }}</span>
        {% endfor %}
      </p>
      {% for r in dm.runs %}
        {% assign t = pf.trajectories | where: 'id', r.id | first %}
        <div class="pf-bar-row pf-mix-row" data-trajectory="{{ r.id }}">
          <span class="pf-bar-label"
            ><span class="pf-swatch" aria-hidden="true"></span><small>{{ r.id }}</small><span class="pf-mix-name">{{ t.short_title }}</span></span
          >
          <span class="pf-mix-track">
            {%- for g in r.segments -%}
              {%- assign c = dm.carriers | where: 'id', g.key | first -%}
              <span
                class="pf-mix-part pf-mix-{{ g.key }}{% if g.value < 2.5 and g.value > -2.5 %} pf-mix-small{% endif %}"
                style="left: {{ g.left }}%; width: {{ g.width }}%"
                title="{{ c.label }}: {{ g.value }} TWh"
              >
                {%- if g.value >= 2.5 %}+{{ g.value | round }}{% endif -%}
              </span>
            {%- endfor -%}
            <span class="pf-mix-axis" style="left: {{ dm.axis_left }}%" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </span>
          <span class="pf-bar-value pf-mix-trade">+{{ r.total }} TWh</span>
        </div>
      {% endfor %}
      <p class="pf-caption">{{ dm.note }}</p>
    </figure>
    <p class="pf-caption">{{ ad.note }}</p>
  </div>

  <div class="pf-model-feedback" id="pf-policy-feedback">
    <h3>Back to the policy assumptions</h3>
    {% include policy-feasibility/feedback-loop.liquid %}
    {% for paragraph in pf.model_application.feedback %}
      <p class="pf-prose">{{ paragraph }}</p>
    {% endfor %}
  </div>

  <div class="pf-drawers">
    {% assign lp = sc.load_profiles %}
    <details class="pf-drawer" id="pf-load-profiles">
      <summary>
        <strong>Electricity use in the model</strong
        ><span>How datacenter projects are weighted, where other demand comes from and how it varies over the day and year</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="pf-drawer-body">
        <p class="pf-prose">{{ ds.text[0] | replace: 'Nordic datacenter tracker', tracker_link | replace: ds.epri.label, epri_link }}</p>
        <p class="pf-prose">
          Electricity use per bidding zone follows the Swedish Energy Agency’s current-policy scenario, broken down by county and sector in
          <a href="https://www.behovskartan.se/">Behovskartan</a>, a tool for exploring Sweden’s future electricity needs developed by AI Sweden. The
          scenario’s own growth in datacenter use after 2025 is removed. It is replaced by the projects in the tracker, weighted as described above.
          Growth after 2025 gets an hourly profile per sector. Electric cars, trucks and rail use typical profiles from Behovskartan. Households and
          services use measured 2023 data, and electric heating follows heat pumps at 2023 temperatures. Industry and hydrogen production are flat.
          Large datacenters use the hourly shape of Norwegian datacenters, with monthly factors from the datacenter data in Behovskartan. Datacenters
          for AI and high-performance computing are flat. There are no open hourly series per sector for Sweden, so households, services and
          datacenters use Norwegian measured data from <a href="https://data.elhub.no/">Elhub</a>, the Norwegian data hub for electricity metering.
          The charts show bidding zone SE3, central Sweden including Stockholm.
        </p>
        <figure class="pf-loadprof" aria-labelledby="pf-loadprof-caption">
          <p class="pf-mix-legend">
            {% for sr in lp.series %}
              <span><span class="pf-key pf-lp-{{ sr.id }}" aria-hidden="true"></span>{{ sr.label }}</span>
            {% endfor %}
          </p>
          <div class="pf-loadprof-panels">
            <div>
              <p class="pf-loadprof-title">A week in January, {{ lp.zone }}</p>
              <svg viewBox="-28 -8 640 232" role="img" aria-label="Hourly load profiles for one week in January">
                {% for t in lp.week_ticks %}
                  <line class="pf-lp-grid{% if t.v == 1 %} pf-lp-one{% endif %}" x1="0" x2="600" y1="{{ t.y }}" y2="{{ t.y }}" />
                  <text class="pf-lp-tick" x="-8" y="{{ t.y | plus: 4 }}" text-anchor="end">{{ t.v }}</text>
                {% endfor %}
                {% for dname in lp.days %}
                  <text class="pf-lp-tick" x="{{ forloop.index0 | times: 600.0 | divided_by: 7 | plus: 42.9 }}" y="222" text-anchor="middle">{{ dname }}</text>
                {% endfor %}
                {% for sr in lp.series %}<path class="pf-lp-line pf-lp-{{ sr.id }}" d="{{ sr.week }}" />{% endfor %}
              </svg>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="pf-loadprof-title">Monthly average, {{ lp.zone }}</p>
              <svg viewBox="-28 -8 340 232" role="img" aria-label="Monthly average of the load profiles">
                {% for t in lp.month_ticks %}
                  <line class="pf-lp-grid{% if t.v == 1 %} pf-lp-one{% endif %}" x1="0" x2="300" y1="{{ t.y }}" y2="{{ t.y }}" />
                  <text class="pf-lp-tick" x="-8" y="{{ t.y | plus: 4 }}" text-anchor="end">{{ t.v }}</text>
                {% endfor %}
                {% for mname in lp.months %}
                  <text class="pf-lp-tick" x="{{ forloop.index0 | times: 300.0 | divided_by: 11 }}" y="222" text-anchor="middle">{{ mname }}</text>
                {% endfor %}
                {% for sr in lp.series %}<path class="pf-lp-line pf-lp-{{ sr.id }}" d="{{ sr.month }}" />{% endfor %}
              </svg>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="pf-caption" id="pf-loadprof-caption">
            Hourly profiles relative to the yearly average (1 = average). Datacenters use almost the same power every hour. Their peak is about 5%
            above the average, and July is about 7% above January because of cooling.
          </p>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </details>
    <details class="pf-drawer" id="pf-model-assumptions">
      <summary><strong>Model and assumptions</strong><span>Generation limits, grid capacity, external prices and nuclear financing</span></summary>
      <div class="pf-drawer-body pf-assumptions">
        <p>The model is {{ sc.model.name }}. {{ sc.model.setup }}</p>
        <dl>
          {% for a in sc.assumptions %}
            <div>
              <dt>{{ a.label }}</dt>
              <dd>{{ a.value }}</dd>
            </div>
          {% endfor %}
        </dl>
        <p>
          In T1b, the model can meet the requirement with offshore wind or with small modular reactors (SMRs). It builds offshore wind, which is
          cheaper in the model, and the permitted offshore parks in SE3 and SE4 have room for most of what the datacenters need. The cost of new
          nuclear follows the Swedish government’s financing inquiry at 7% real interest. It does not include the state loans and price guarantees for
          new nuclear that Sweden adopted in 2025, which would lower the cost to the developer. New large reactors are assumed not to be in operation
          by 2035.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>
